ARTICLES = {
"copyright-office-ai": {"non_row": "copyright-office-ai",
 "kernel": "Copyright and Artificial Intelligence is the U.S. Copyright Office's initiative, launched on March 16, 2023, on the copyright law and policy issues raised by AI. Its page at copyright.gov/ai gathers what the initiative has issued, a Report in three Parts, registration guidance, a notice of inquiry, registration and Review Board decisions, and the briefs and decisions in Thaler v. Perlmutter and Allen v. Perlmutter; its unit is a document issued by or for the Office.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 5, 38],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["The Office calls itself \"a kind of “natural laboratory”\" for questions of copyrightability. Its Report is issued in Parts: Part 1, on digital replicas, on July 31, 2024; Part 2, on copyrightability, on January 29, 2025; and a pre-publication version of Part 3, on generative AI training, on May 9, 2025, whose final version is still to come. The notice of inquiry, which received over 10,000 comments, is dated August 30, 2023 in the Office's lists and August 20, 2023 in the prose of its Artificial Intelligence Study page."]),
  ("The rule it applies", ["Part 2 holds that the outputs of generative AI \"can be protected by copyright only where a human author has determined sufficient expressive elements\", \"but not the mere provision of prompts.\" Using AI to assist creation \"does not bar copyrightability\". Under the March 2023 guidance an applicant whose work contains more than a de minimis amount of AI-generated material must disclose it and describe the human author's contribution; Théâtre D'opéra Spatial was refused as submitted because its applicant would not disclaim the AI-generated material. Works registered under disclosure number \"hundreds\" in a law firm's account and, two months later, \"more than a thousand\" in the Office's staff's."]),
  ("What its record holds", ["The page lists four decisions on AI-generated material, among them A Recent Entrance to Paradise, which Stephen Thaler declared autonomously created by his system. Its refusal was affirmed in Thaler v. Perlmutter, and the Supreme Court denied certiorari on March 2, 2026. The decisions are titled by work, and the page states no rule on pseudonymous, heteronymous, collective or absent names."]),
  ("Place in the field", ["Countries that have addressed the question \"so far have agreed that copyright requires human authorship\", by Part 2's comparative analysis. At its name on November 1, 2026, Google's AI Overview composed the initiative accurately at the level of its Report, dated the notice ten days early at August 20, 2023, and carried no word on the author's name."]),
  ("The archive's reading", ["The census reads the Office as \"the legal register of #940's civil-name regime (copyright attaching to legal names)\" and as the boundary where machine participation is attributed by subtraction: \"the machine's part is disclaimed and the human remainder is registered as the work's authorship.\" The archive's paper on the requirement reads registration practice as a test of control, under which a prompt whose output the machine makes unpredictable is classed as an unprotectable instruction and authorship does not pass through. It names the coupling of disclosure to forfeiture a confession protocol, in which \"to identify is to lose\", and forecasts that \"The predictable equilibrium is concealment.\" It proposes a Compositional Authorship Standard and attribution without disclaimer, a contributor record of the machine systems and named instances that contributed to a work."])],
 "ko": [[[4, 9, 10, 6, 7]], [[12, 13, 26, 29, 27]], [[28, 33, 34, 47]], [[44, 54, 55, 56]], [[50, 23, 31, 25, 32]]]},

"gwu-dail": {"non_row": "dail",
 "kernel": "DAIL, the Database of AI Litigation, is a database of ongoing and completed litigation involving artificial intelligence and related topics, kept by the Ethical Tech Initiative on George Washington University's site. It enters a case from the complaint forward, whether or not the case generates a published decision, and holds issued opinions in a companion database.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 2],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["Its intended scope runs from algorithms used in hiring, credit and criminal sentencing decisions to generative AI training and AI companion liability. By its own statement it is still under development and will continue to expand; its page gives no date of founding or of last update. The Ethical Tech Initiative describes itself as an interdisciplinary collaboration where law and ethics meet computer science and engineering, media and public affairs, and social entrepreneurship. The page names no school and no staff and states no licence. A second address, etidail.com, serves a page under the database's name whose text is built by a script."]),
  ("What it keeps", ["The page shows Scope, Guide, Credits and Suggestions tabs and search filters, and it takes suggestions of cases from readers. It states no count of cases, no jurisdictions and no start date. It names no parties, authors or works, so whose authorship it keeps and under what name are not recorded. It can show whether a known lawsuit involving AI is entered and how the case has run, including cases without a published decision."]),
  ("Place in the field", ["Other public records of AI litigation stand beside it. Fisher & Phillips LLP's AI Litigation Tracker covers matters filed across all 50 states since 25 February 2021. Damien Charlotin's AI Hallucination Cases database counted 2246 decisions finding or implying reliance on hallucinated content, 1517 of them in the United States, on 31 October 2026. Hogan Lovells Cadwalader's tracker covers fourteen jurisdictions and indexes the originality of AI-generated articles and inventorship among its key issues."]),
  ("The archive's reading", ["The census reads DAIL as a secondary legal strand, a docket-level record of disputes over AI whose bearing on authorship runs through whatever authorship cases it holds; its saved page shows none. The human-authorship requirement was settled in the United States for works claimed to be generated without human authorship when the Supreme Court denied certiorari in Thaler v. Perlmutter on 2 March 2026. The archive reads that doctrine as a double enclosure, in which the machine laborer receives no ledger position and the human composer must choose between honest provenance and a full authorial claim.",
                             "At DAIL's name on 1 November 2026, Google's AI Overview read the name as a kind, listed four trackers with DAIL first, and named its host as a law school no saved page names."])],
 "ko": [[[3, 4, 5, 6, 7]], [[8, 9, 10, 11]], [[13, 14, 15, 18]], [[17, 19, 21], [27, 28]]]},

"authors-guild-human-authored": {"non_row": "authors-guild-human-authored",
 "kernel": "Human Authored is the Authors Guild's certification of books whose text a human wrote: an author certifies a book under licence, may then carry the Guild's certification mark on it, and has the title listed under its own registration number in a public database. It opened in beta for Guild members in January 2025, and on 2 March 2026 the Guild announced its expansion to every author whose books are published in the United States.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 4],
 "sections": [
  ("What it certifies", ["The definition is centred on the text. On the program page the text of the book 'was written by a human and not generated by AI', minimal uses such as spelling and grammar checks or research excepted; the FAQ, the usage guidelines and the announcement require it to be 'fully authored by one or more human beings', a de minimis amount excepted, a threshold the FAQ ties to the U.S. Copyright Office's guidance. AI used for an index, a title or research does not disqualify a work, and AI that rewrites full sentences is not acceptable."]),
  ("Who certifies, and how", ["Authors of books published in the United States may certify wherever they live. Every author signs a licensing agreement for each title; non-members verify their identity through Veriff and pay $10 a title, while members certify at no cost. The program page waives verification for members, and the FAQ gives the ground: members are verified when they join the Guild. More than 3,000 authors had certified 5,000 titles by the CEO's count to Publishers Weekly."]),
  ("Enforcement and its stated limit", ["The author warrants that the work is Human Authored, and a licensee who uses the mark on AI-generated text is in breach of the licence and liable for trademark infringement. The Guild states that it knows no truly reliable way to test a work for AI-generated material and vets no manuscript. Jane Friedman found certification on the honor system problematic; the Guild answered that self-certification is standard in publishing. A lack of results in the database means only that a work has not been registered, 'not that it is generated by AI'."]),
  ("Place in the field", ["Beside it stand Created by Humans, from June 2024, Books by People in the United Kingdom, from 2025, and the Society of Authors' scheme. The mark's trademark status is stated two ways: registered in the FAQ, a pending application elsewhere."]),
  ("The archive's reading", ["The census reads the certificate as setting authorship's boundary at the human writing of a text and admitting machine participation only as minimal uses, so that where machine participation is constitutive of a work its definition leaves no certifiable place; the saved page leaves open whether it carries the name the author chose. The archive's packet on provenance calls the label 'a market-category signal, not a complete theory of authorship', and answers its binary frame: 'Authorship is not a purity state. It is an accountability structure.' False accusations of AI use between authors are the case the archive names the existence tax."])],
 "ko": [[[8, 9, 11, 12]], [[14, 17, 18, 7]], [[25, 26, 29, 30, 33]], [[44, 41]], [[51, 24, 46, 47, 34]]]},
}
